<header><p class="st-kicker">margraviate · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Marquisate of Saluzzo</h1><p class="st-meta">1142 CE – 1548 CE · capital Saluzzo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Alpine marquisate in south-western Piedmont that balanced between Savoy, France and the Dauphiné for four centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The marquisate was formed in 1142 when the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-del-vasto">Boniface del Vasto</a> were divided among his sons, and Manfred I received Saluzzo and the upper valleys of the Po, Varaita and Maira.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The marquises ruled from their castle at Saluzzo over a small state of mountain valleys and plain. Squeezed between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-savoy">County of Savoy</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dauphine-of-viennois">Dauphiné</a>, they often sought French protection. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-iii-of-saluzzo">Marquis Thomas III</a> wrote the allegorical romance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-chevalier-errant">Le Chevalier errant</a> around 1400. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-ii-marquis-of-saluzzo">Ludovico II</a> opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buco-di-viso">Buco di Viso</a> tunnel under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> around 1480 to trade salt and goods with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-provence">Provence</a>, and the town flourished as a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> court.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After quarrels in the ruling family, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france">Henry II of France</a> annexed the marquisate in 1548. It was occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-emmanuel-i-duke-of-savoy">Charles Emmanuel I</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy</a> in 1588 and formally ceded to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-savoy">Savoy</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lyon-1601">Treaty of Lyon</a> in 1601.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/marquisate-of-saluzzo.jpg" alt="Marquisate of Saluzzo" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Dekodrak · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Marquistate_of_Saluzzo_(14th_century-c._1507).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1142 CE – 1548 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>margraviate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saluzzo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Manfred I of Saluzzo, Thomas III of Saluzzo, Ludovico II of Saluzzo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Piedmontese, Occitan, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Saluzzo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1345&amp;focus=marquisate-of-saluzzo" aria-label="Marquisate of Saluzzo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4859 599 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Marquisate of Saluzzo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5179 855L5218 839M5179 855L5054 728M5179 855L5140 827"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5218 839h0M5054 728h0M5140 827h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5179 855h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5179 855h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Saluzzo. <a href="/atlas?year=1345&amp;focus=marquisate-of-saluzzo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
